  <section id="why" aria-labelledby="why-h">
    <h2 id="why-h">Why Height Differs Between Countries</h2>
    <p>Within any country, most of the difference between individuals is genetic. Between countries, environment matters a great deal. Children who get enough protein and calories, clean water and good health care grow closer to their genetic potential. That is why heights rose by 10 to 20&nbsp;cm in many countries over the 20th century as living standards improved, and why they have stalled where childhood nutrition has not improved.</p>
    <p>Height is also linked with health: taller populations tend to have lower risk of heart disease but slightly higher risk of some cancers. Neither tall nor short is &ldquo;better&rdquo;; height mostly reflects childhood conditions.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="you" aria-labelledby="you-h">
    <h2 id="you-h">How Your Height Compares</h2>
    <p>Use the tool at the top to see where you would rank in any country. For your exact percentile among U.S. adults of your sex and age, use the <a href="/height-percentile-calculator/">height percentile calculator</a>. To predict how tall a child will be, try the <a href="/height-calculator/">height calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Average Height by Country FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Which country has the tallest people?</h3></summary><p>The Netherlands. Dutch 19-year-old men average 183.8 cm (6 ft 0.4 in) and women 170.4 cm (5 ft 7.1 in), the tallest in the world in the NCD Risk Factor Collaboration’s 2019 estimates. Montenegro, Estonia, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Iceland and Denmark follow closely.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Which country has the shortest people?</h3></summary><p>For men, Timor-Leste has the lowest average at 160.1 cm (5 ft 3 in), followed by Laos, Papua New Guinea and the Solomon Islands. For women, Guatemala has the lowest average at about 150.9 cm (4 ft 11.4 in).</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the average height in the United States?</h3></summary><p>U.S. 19-year-olds average 176.9 cm (5 ft 9.6 in) for men and 163.3 cm (5 ft 4.3 in) for women in the 2019 international estimates. Across all U.S. adults, CDC data give 5 ft 9 in for men and 5 ft 3.5 in for women; older adults are slightly shorter.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the average height in the world?</h3></summary><p>Worldwide, the average young man is roughly 171 to 172 cm (5 ft 7.5 in) and the average young woman roughly 159 to 160 cm (5 ft 3 in), with a gap of about 20 cm between the tallest and shortest countries.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Why are some countries taller than others?</h3></summary><p>Genes set the range, but childhood nutrition, health and living conditions decide how much of that potential people reach. Countries where children eat well and suffer fewer infections have grown much taller over the past century; Dutch men are roughly 20 cm taller than their great-great-grandparents were.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Are people getting taller?</h3></summary><p>In most countries, yes, but growth has stalled or slightly reversed in some. South Korean women and Iranian men gained more than 16 cm over the 20th century, while heights in many sub-Saharan African countries have barely changed since the 1980s.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is 6 feet tall in other countries?</h3></summary><p>6 ft (182.9 cm) is taller than the average young man in every country except the Netherlands (183.8 cm) and Montenegro (183.3 cm), where it is just below average.</p></details>
  </section>

